/// A recurring slot: this weekday, between these clock times, from this date
/// to that one.
///
/// ─── Two prototypes, one week apart, three weekday encodings ──────────────
///
/// calendly `AvailabilityRule` and `AvailabilityOverride`; connectteam
/// `AvailabilityWindow` and `ShiftRule`; outside the repo cal.com
/// `Availability` and hrms `ShiftType`. The columns agree. The weekday does
/// not:
///
///   calendly     weekday Int @gte(1) @lte(7)       — a number box in every form
///   connectteam  weekday Weekday  (mon … sun)      — a picker
///   cal.com      days Int[]                        — one row, many days
///
/// That is the whole reason the enum ships with the trait. An `enum` reaches
/// the browser as a set a control renders and reaches the table as a CHECK;
/// an `Int` reaches both as a number and every form writes its own label
/// map. The two-letter-plus-one spelling is ISO 8601's week order starting
/// Monday, which is also what `Date.getDay()` is NOT — a host converting from
/// JavaScript's Sunday-first integer does it in one place, the hook, rather
/// than in every reader.
///
/// cal.com's array is a different shape — one row for Mon/Wed/Fri — and is
/// three rows here. Three rows is more honest: `@@exclude` and `@@unique` can
/// see three rows and cannot see inside an array.
///
/// ─── Clock times are strings, deliberately ────────────────────────────────
///
/// `09:00` is not an instant. It is the same wall-clock time every week,
/// which lands on a different instant each time the zone's offset moves.
/// `String @time` keeps it a wall-clock value; a `DateTime` would freeze one
/// week's offset into every week. The zone it is read in belongs to the host
/// (the schedule's, the site's) and is where `Interval`'s derivation looks it
/// up.
///
/// ─── What is deliberately NOT here ────────────────────────────────────────
///
/// **No `crossesMidnight`.** connectteam carries it for a night shift that
/// ends tomorrow, and it is the hard case every other instance silently has.
/// It is left out here because the right fix is in the derivation, not the
/// row: an end before its start means "next day", and a flag that restates
/// that is a second answer the check below would have to be relaxed for. A
/// host that disagrees overrides the check.
///
/// **No `available Boolean`.** calendly's override row says "this date is
/// closed" with the same columns plus a flag. That is a different row —
/// an exception to a rule rather than a rule — and belongs in the host under
/// its own name.
enum Weekday { mon tue wed thu fri sat sun }

trait Window {
  weekday  Weekday

  /// Wall-clock, in the host's zone. `@time` validates the shape.
  startAt  String  @time
  endAt    String  @time

  /// The dates the rule holds between. Both optional: an open-ended standing
  /// rule is the common case, and a rule with a `toDate` is how a schedule
  /// changes without a row being edited under the past that depended on it.
  fromDate String? @date
  toDate   String? @date

  @@check("startAt < endAt", "A window ends after it starts")
  @@check("fromDate IS NULL OR toDate IS NULL OR fromDate <= toDate", "A rule ends on or after the day it starts")
}
